# ComposeUX CYD Line-by-Line + DMA-Overlap + Dirty-Band Renderer [SPEC:CXC-LINE]

> Lift #2 from the Slint comparative analysis, technique-only: two band buffers instead of one, so the CPU renders band N+1 while band N streams to the ILI9341 over DMA (compute/transfer overlap); a mode selector that picks full-framebuffer pointer-swap on PSRAM/scan-out boards and strict line-by-line band transfer on the classic CYD; and dirty-band tracking fed by the existing `request_redraw → present_partial(rects)` damage model so unchanged rows are never re-rendered or re-transferred. This attacks the single worst measured bottleneck — the [CXC-RM-M5] floor (empty-scene band render 56 ms / 17.9 fps; 10-widget dashboard 2.7 fps; "animation on the CYD needs dirty-band rendering we have not built") — and it is the on-device gate for Phase D animation ([CXC-BIND-STATES]).

---

## 1. Intent

### [CXC-LINE-INTENT] Why This Lift, and Exactly What Is Being Lifted

- definition: Lift the ALGORITHM of Slint's MCU software renderer into the existing `ESP32Canvas` band renderer: (1) a second one-band buffer + asynchronous band transfer so compute and DMA overlap; (2) selection between a full-framebuffer path (capable boards) and a strict line-by-line/band path (constrained boards); (3) dirty-band tracking that skips unchanged rows. The classic CYD (ESP32 + ILI9341 240×320, 16-scanline / 7,680-byte band buffer, 20 bands/frame) is the target and the perf FLOOR of the whole system ([CXC-RM-M5] board-family).
- rationale: The measured floor ([CXC-RM-M5] perf-floor, 2026-07-07): empty-scene full-frame band render 56 ms (17.9 fps); 10-widget gauge dashboard 367 ms (2.7 fps); and the explicit gap "ANIMATION needs dirty-band rendering (unbuilt) or the sprite path." Two of the three techniques directly attack this: dirty-band SKIP removes the transfer cost of idle rows (the empty/idle-frame win), and DMA overlap hides per-band compute under the transfer of the prior band (the compute-heavy-band win).
- clean-room: The technique is taken from public Slint documentation (the porting-to-microcontrollers write-up: two one-line buffers, render-ahead while the prior line DMAs, dirty-line tracking). NO Slint source is vendored — Slint is GPLv3 and this firmware is non-GPL. See [CXC-LINE-SCOPE].
- key-fact-current-state: The as-built `ESP32Canvas` ([CXC-RM-M5]) already has ONE `_band_buf` and both a full-frame path (`render_frame`) and a dirty entry point (`render_bands(first, last)`, [CXC-ER-RENDER]). What is MISSING and what this lift adds: the SECOND buffer + async transfer (there is no compute/transfer overlap today — `DisplayDriver::transfer_band` is blocking), and the damage→dirty-band mapping + clean-band skip that makes `render_bands` fire from real damage.
- status: planned
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-LINE]

---

## 2. The Seam (Reuse-by-Reference)

### [CXC-LINE-SEAM] No New Public Seam — the Existing Damage Model Suffices

- finding: The existing public seam is sufficient; this lift proposes NO new public Canvas seam. The damage model already reaches from widget to backend: a widget calls `request_redraw()` (optionally with its rect), the engine unions damage, and the Python `Canvas` ABC exposes `present_partial(rects)` — whose contract already states an "ESP32 framebuffer overrides to push only the dirty window over SPI (its single biggest refresh win)" (`python/composeio/render/canvas.py`). On the C++ tier the same seam is `render_bands(first, last)` ([CXC-ER-RENDER]). This lift WIRES that seam; it does not add one.
- reuse-by-reference (extend, never fork): the build MUST extend these existing files/contracts, not replace them:
  - `cyd/src/canvas_esp32.{h,cpp}` — `ESP32Canvas`, `_band_buf`, `render_frame`, `render_bands`, `kBandHeight` (16), the 7,680-byte band buffer.
  - `cyd/include/board_profile.h` — `DisplayDriver::transfer_band(buf, y_start, y_end)` (the band transfer seam) and the zero-copy `scanout_framebuffers()` / `present_scanout()` seam already present for RGB dot-clock panels.
  - `python/composeio/render/canvas.py` — `present_partial(rects)` (the damage seam's desktop expression; the on-device path mirrors its semantics, no signature change).
  - `python/composeio/render/widget.py` — `request_redraw` and the engine damage union that feeds it.
- one-internal-addition (NOT a public seam): compute/transfer overlap requires the band transfer to be non-blocking. The build MUST add an asynchronous transfer capability to the `DisplayDriver` band seam — a `transfer_band_async(buf, y_start, y_end)` + `wait_band_transfer()` pair (or an equivalent completion handle) — as an OPTIONAL driver capability with a default that falls back to today's blocking `transfer_band`. This is an addition to the internal board-driver interface, degradable and board-local; it is NOT a change to the public widget/Canvas contract.
- test: when a widget calls `request_redraw()` with a rect and nothing else changes, then only the band(s) intersecting that rect are re-rendered and transferred (verified against the damage union), proving the seam is the existing one, unmodified in signature.
- status: planned
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-LINE]

---

## 3. Technique

### [CXC-LINE-OVERLAP] Double-Buffered Band + DMA Compute/Transfer Overlap

- definition: Allocate TWO band buffers (ping-pong) instead of one. The render loop MUST, for band N (N ≥ 1): kick the asynchronous transfer of band N−1 (already composited into the other buffer), then compute band N into the current buffer while that transfer runs, then swap. The frame's last band is transferred and the loop waits its completion before returning. This overlaps CPU compositing with SPI DMA, so per-frame wall time trends toward `max(Σ compute, Σ transfer) + one band` rather than their sum.
- default-buffer-count: 2 (double buffer). Each buffer is `width × kBandHeight × 2` bytes = 7,680 B on the classic CYD (240×16×2); the second buffer costs +7,680 B of DMA-capable/internal SRAM against the ~199 KB free baseline ([CXC-RM-M5] heap).
- MUST — buffer-reuse safety: a buffer MUST NOT be written by a compute pass while its previous transfer is in flight. The loop MUST `wait_band_transfer()` on a buffer before reusing it. Violating this is the tearing/corruption failure mode; the wait is the invariant.
- MUST — degrade, never fail: if a second DMA-capable band buffer cannot be allocated at `create_surface`, the canvas MUST fall back to single-buffer blocking transfer (today's `render_frame` behavior) and log the degradation. No fatal.
- SHOULD — transfer-bound honesty: on the classic CYD the EMPTY full-frame floor (56 ms) is SPI-bandwidth-bound (240×320×16 bit ÷ 27 MHz ≈ 45 ms of transfer alone); overlap alone CANNOT beat SPI bandwidth for a full-frame transfer. Overlap's win is on compute-heavy bands (e.g. the gauge, ~44 ms) where compute ≳ transfer; the empty/idle win comes from [CXC-LINE-DIRTY] skip, not from overlap. The build MUST NOT claim an empty-full-frame speedup from overlap.
- test: when a compute-heavy full-frame redraw runs with double buffering, then measured wall time is ≤ (Σ compute + one-band transfer) and strictly less than the single-buffer sum, verified by the on-device timing harness; when only one buffer can be allocated, then the frame still renders correctly via the blocking fallback.
- depends-on: [CXC-RM-M5]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-LINE]

### [CXC-LINE-MODES] Full-Framebuffer vs Strict Line-by-Line Mode Selection

- definition: The renderer MUST support both a full-framebuffer mode and a strict line-by-line/band mode, and select between them from board capability — not from a per-scene flag. Selection key: `DisplayDriver::scanout_framebuffers(out, max)` — a driver returning ≥ 2 scan-out framebuffers (PSRAM/RGB dot-clock boards, e.g. the named Qualia S3 sub-tier, [CXC-RM-M5] board-family) selects the full-framebuffer path (composite into the back buffer, present by POINTER SWAP via `present_scanout`, zero PSRAM→PSRAM copy); a driver returning < 2 (the classic CYD SPI/ILI9341) selects the strict band path ([CXC-LINE-OVERLAP] + `transfer_band`).
- default-mode: band (strict line-by-line) — the classic-CYD floor and the universal-deployability target. Full-framebuffer is the capable-board upgrade, entered ONLY when the driver advertises scan-out buffers.
- MUST — full-fb refusal on incapable boards: full-framebuffer mode MUST NOT be entered on a board whose driver returns < 2 scan-out framebuffers (no PSRAM/scan-out); such a board is band-only. This prevents an OOM on a config authored for a capable board.
- SHOULD — mode is transparent to widgets: a widget's `draw()` against the `Canvas` contract is byte-identical in either mode (band clipping vs full surface is a canvas-internal concern); the pixel gate ([CXC-R-C1]) MUST hold across both.
- note: the classic-CYD path is the deliverable of THIS lift; the Qualia full-framebuffer BUILD is out of scope ([CXC-LINE-SCOPE]) — this node defines only the selection seam so "add a board profile" is all a capable board needs.
- test: when the linked board profile advertises ≥ 2 scan-out framebuffers, then the full-fb pointer-swap path is selected; when it advertises < 2, then the band path is selected and full-fb is refused; a widget renders pixel-identically (within [CXC-R-C1] tolerance) under either selection.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-LINE]

### [CXC-LINE-DIRTY] Dirty-Band Tracking Wired to the Damage Model

- definition: Map the engine's damage (the union of `present_partial(rects)` / `request_redraw` rects) to the set of dirty BAND indices, then re-render and re-transfer ONLY those bands (via the existing `render_bands(first, last)`), skipping clean bands entirely. Band index for a scanline `y` is `y / kBandHeight`; a rect `[y0, y1)` marks bands `floor(y0/kBandHeight) .. floor((y1−1)/kBandHeight)` inclusive. Overlapping/adjacent dirty band runs are coalesced into contiguous `render_bands` calls to keep SPI window-setting overhead low.
- MUST — clean-band correctness (no global clear on partial present): on a partial present the renderer MUST NOT clear the whole panel; clean bands retain their prior on-panel contents (ILI9341 GRAM is persistent). A partial frame clears and repaints ONLY dirty bands. Clearing globally would blank the clean regions — the correctness invariant.
- MUST — idle no-op: an empty damage set (no widget requested redraw) presents NOTHING — no clear, no transfer. The idle-frame cost is the damage check only. This is the empty/idle win that raises the effective animation refresh ceiling far above the 17.9 fps full-frame floor.
- MUST — full-damage degradation: a damage set covering all bands (or a full-frame `request_redraw()`) degrades to the [CXC-LINE-OVERLAP] full path and MUST NOT be slower than today's `render_frame` (overlap can only help or tie).
- MUST — bounds clamping: damage rects MUST be clamped to `[0, height)` / `[0, width)`; a rect crossing a band boundary marks BOTH bands dirty.
- SHOULD — animation amortization: for a localized animated region (e.g. a gauge needle occupying ≤ 3 bands), amortized per-frame cost is proportional to the dirty band count, not the 20-band frame — the mechanism that makes Phase D tweens viable ([CXC-BIND-STATES]).
- test: when a single small widget requests redraw of its rect, then exactly the intersecting band(s) are re-rendered+transferred and clean bands are untouched (verified by a per-band transfer counter and a pixel-diff showing clean regions unchanged); when damage is empty, then zero bands transfer; when damage covers the frame, then the full overlap path runs at ≤ the full-frame baseline.
- see-also: [CXC-ER-RENDER]
- depends-on: [SPEC:CXC-RENDER]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-LINE]

