## 4. Acceptance Targets

### [CXC-LINE-TARGETS] Two-Tier Verdict — Machine Signals AND an On-Device Glass Gate

- definition: The lift is accepted against measurable machine criteria AND a final human on-device glass gate. Machine-green is necessary but NOT sufficient — a timing harness and a pixel-diff cannot see band tearing, stale-clean-band artifacts, or flicker the way an eye on the panel can. Final acceptance is David's on-device look.
- verify.machine:
  - M1 idle-floor: a static scene with ONE small animating region (e.g. a gauge needle, ≤ 3 bands) sustains ≥ 30 fps on the classic CYD (only dirty bands re-render+transfer), vs today's whole-frame repaint. This is the headline Phase-D-viability number and it clears the 17.9 fps floor by design.
  - M2 idle no-op: a fully static frame (no damage) costs < 5 ms/frame (damage check only, zero transfer), vs 56 ms for a full redraw today.
  - M3 overlap: on a compute-heavy full-frame redraw (the 10-widget gauge dashboard, 367 ms baseline), double-buffer wall time is strictly less than the single-buffer sum and trends toward `max(Σ compute, Σ transfer)`; report the measured full-frame time and the per-band overlap factor.
  - M4 correctness/pixel gate: partial-present frames pass the existing pixel gate ([CXC-R-C1], `scripts/cyd_config_crosscheck.py`) — clean regions byte-stable, dirty regions matching the Skia reference within the AA/RGB565 class; no global-clear blanking.
  - M5 no-regression: `render_frame` full-frame output is unchanged (goldens hold); single-buffer fallback renders correctly when the second buffer cannot allocate.
- verify.human: on the attached CYD hardware, an animated gauge/needle tween (and a Phase-D `[CXC-BIND-STATES]` transition) is visually smooth — no band tearing, no stale-band seams at dirty/clean boundaries, no flicker. This on-device glass gate is the final verdict; machine-green ≠ glass-green.
- change: "The [CXC-RM-M5] perf-floor language 'ANIMATION needs dirty-band rendering (unbuilt)' and the M5 'DEFERRED ... dirty-band rendering' item are sharpened from an unbuilt/deferred note into a specified deliverable with measurable targets, and the technique is widened beyond bare dirty-band to add double-buffered DMA compute/transfer overlap and explicit full-fb/line-by-line mode selection."
- context_for_change:
  - prior_state: "[CXC-RM-M5] measured and named the floor (56 ms/17.9 fps empty; 2.7 fps dashboard) and logged dirty-band rendering as unbuilt/deferred — the path to on-device animation was identified but not specified."
  - reasoning: "The Slint comparative analysis (Lift #2) supplies a proven-in-production technique for exactly this floor; specifying it now converts a roadmap gap into a build-ready design and unblocks Phase D animation on the constrained tier."
  - rejected_alternative: "Rely on the sprite path ([CXC-R-C18], baked CI16 frames) for all on-device motion — rejected: sprites cover pre-baked non-interactive motion, not bus-driven/tweened widget state ([CXC-BIND-STATES]) whose value is unknown at bake time."
  - invalidating_assumption: "That the classic CYD stays SPI-transfer-bound at 27 MHz with a 16-scanline band. If the SPI clock, band height, or panel bus (e.g. a parallel/RGB panel) changes, re-derive the transfer/compute balance and the overlap win — the dirty-band skip still holds, the overlap arithmetic does not."
- see-also: [CXC-BIND-STATES]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-LINE]

---

## 5. Error & Boundary Conditions

### [CXC-LINE-EDGE] The Non-Happy-Path Contract

- second-buffer OOM: at `create_surface`, if a second DMA-capable band buffer cannot be allocated, fall back to single-buffer blocking transfer and log; never fatal ([CXC-LINE-OVERLAP]).
- DMA in-flight reuse: a compute pass MUST block on `wait_band_transfer()` before writing a buffer whose prior transfer is unfinished; the wait is mandatory, not best-effort.
- transfer completion timeout: `wait_band_transfer()` MUST time out (bounded) and fall back / report rather than wedge — precedent: the M5 capture-push infinite-wedge bug that was fixed to fail cleanly ([CXC-RM-M5]).
- empty damage set: present nothing (no clear, no transfer) — the idle no-op ([CXC-LINE-DIRTY]).
- full / all-band damage: degrade to the full overlap path; MUST NOT exceed the `render_frame` baseline time ([CXC-LINE-DIRTY]).
- out-of-bounds / boundary-crossing rects: clamp to panel bounds; a rect crossing a band boundary marks both bands dirty ([CXC-LINE-DIRTY]).
- full-fb mode on an incapable board: refuse; use band mode ([CXC-LINE-MODES]).
- WiFi/MQTT SPI + core coexistence: band DMA MUST coexist with the WiFi/MQTT tasks (band rendering's original motivation was WiFi coexistence, [CXC-RM-M5]); the async transfer MUST NOT starve the MQTT task or monopolize the shared SPI host — arbitrate the DMA channel/bus, do not busy-wait the CPU during transfer (the point of async is to yield compute to the next band, not to spin).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-LINE]

---

## 6. Open Decision (For Ratify)

### [DD-CXC-LINE-MODE] Default Double-Buffer Enablement Policy on the Classic CYD

- alternatives:
  - option-a (recommended): always enable double-band + async overlap on the classic CYD; +7,680 B of ~199 KB free is affordable and overlap+skip is the whole point. A `board_profile` override disables it for a board where SRAM is genuinely tight.
  - option-b: make it an opt-in `board_profile` flag (default off) — safest for heap, but leaves the classic CYD (the floor we are trying to raise) on the slow path by default.
  - option-c: heap-probe at boot and enable when free DMA-capable SRAM exceeds a headroom threshold — adaptive, but adds a boot-time branch and non-deterministic behavior across configs.
- rationale: option-a raises the floor on the exact tier the lift targets, with a cheap, characterized SRAM cost and an explicit escape hatch; option-c's non-determinism fights the pixel/perf gate's reproducibility.
- invalidating_assumption: "That DMA-capable SRAM headroom stays ≳ 16 KB on the classic-CYD baseline after WiFi + MQTT + the decoded-image budget (≤ ~102 KB sprite budget, [CXC-RM-M5]). If a config's image/heap budget squeezes below that, fall back to single-buffer for that config."
- status: decided
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-LINE]

---

## 7. Scope

### [CXC-LINE-SCOPE] Explicitly In and Out

- in: the classic-CYD band renderer technique — second band buffer + async transfer overlap, full-fb/line-by-line mode-selection seam, damage→dirty-band mapping + clean-band skip, and the measurable acceptance targets.
- out — no GPL code: technique/design ONLY, lifted clean-room from public Slint documentation. NO Slint (GPLv3) source is vendored into this non-GPL firmware. This is the critical caveat of Lift #2.
- out — the Qualia full-framebuffer BUILD: this spec defines the mode-selection SEAM ([CXC-LINE-MODES]); building the Qualia S3 profile + its full-fb scan-out path is a separate deliverable ([CXC-RM-M5] board-family).
- out — live raster transforms: rotate/scale stay documented no-ops on this tier ([CXC-R-DRAW]); a band-renderer inverse-mapping rotate is separate M5+ work.
- out — the desktop damage path: Skia dirty-region on desktop is already operational ([CXC-R-ENGINE]); this lift is the on-device realization of the same seam.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-LINE]

